0ZWpDUG41THd1VTJuNFd3bg?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 8.87, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": 11.01, "poll_sd": 4.66, "poll_weight": 0.54, "fundamentals": {"margin": 7.32, "lean": -1.0, "environment": 7.65, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.67}, "rating_margin": 9.91, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.63, "baseline_margin": 10.02, "analyst_adjustment": -0.52, "key_factors": ["Cooper leads polling averages by roughly 10 points with consistent double-digit surveys", "Nearly all expert ratings call the seat a Likely or better Democratic flip", "Prediction markets price Cooper's win probability at 96-97%", "Cooper has a large fundraising edge over Whatley in an open seat"], "rationale": "Polling averages put Cooper up about 10 points, expert raters overwhelmingly call the race Likely D, and markets price a Cooper win near 97%, all pointing to a clear but not absolute Democratic advantage in this open seat. A Likely D label fits better than Safe D because a handful of raters sit at Lean D and the margin leaves room for late movement.", "watch": "A late Republican consolidation around Whatley, aided by Vance's rallies and outside spending, could narrow the margin toward single digits.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's Senate seat is open after Thom Tillis's retirement, and Democrat Roy Cooper faces Republican Michael Whatley. Cooper, a former two-term governor, has led every recent poll, with margins ranging from about six to fourteen points and an average near ten. He has also vastly outraised Whatley, though Republican outside groups have spent heavily against him.\n\nExpert raters overwhelmingly call the race a Likely Democratic flip, with a couple at Lean D and one at Solid D, and prediction markets price Cooper's win chances in the mid-90s. Republicans have retreated from the state publicly, with Vance campaigning for Whatley in the final week. The race still leans clearly to Cooper,